    [38;2;197;201;199mYou're right, per the mod docs. A mod can message a finished subagent by its id, and the[39m
    [38;2;197;201;199mengine wakes that same agent from its transcript with the message. A mod also sees each[39m
    [38;2;197;201;199mworkflow agent start and learns its id.[39m

    [38;2;197;201;199mSo the route: the mod records each workflow reviewer's id as it starts. When a review needs[39m
    [38;2;197;201;199manother pass, the script calls a small tool the mod provides, which messages that same[39m
    [38;2;197;201;199mreviewer and hands back its answer. The reviewer comes back warm, with everything it[39m
    [38;2;197;201;199malready read.[39m

    [38;2;197;201;199mOne fact is open. The docs say workflow agents don't appear in the mod's agent list, so I[39m
    [38;2;197;201;199mdon't know yet whether the engine accepts a workflow agent's id for that message. A tiny[39m
    [38;2;197;201;199mtest mod settles it: a workflow with one agent, then the mod wakes it with a follow-up.[39m
    [38;2;197;201;199mShould I build that test?[39m

 [48;2;67;70;78m   [38;2;197;201;199msure try it with a haiku agents[39m                                                              [48;2;47;48;54m

